Token definition verification for positive-css-var-2.diff
Repo: Shopify/polaris (polaris.shopify.com docs/marketing subsite) @ commit
      6e958b1c597fcc98dadc2d24d8e7c29a397e20c5
File: polaris.shopify.com/src/components/Button/Button.module.scss, line 29
Removed literal: #008060
Added token: var(--primary)

Unlike positive-css-var.diff (primer/css, resolved via the externally
published @primer/primitives npm package), this token is defined IN-REPO:
the polaris.shopify.com docs/marketing subsite ships its own small CSS
custom-property theme (light/dark mode) directly in
polaris.shopify.com/src/styles/globals.scss, not via the separately
published @shopify/polaris-tokens package. This is, if anything, a MORE
airtight resolution than an npm-pinned one -- there is zero version-pinning
ambiguity: the token's value is read directly from the exact parent
commit's tree via `git show <parent>:<path>`.

  $ git show 1324b3ccc83df99610737e3a0ae338a4c7171de6:polaris.shopify.com/src/styles/globals.scss | grep -n -- "--primary:"
    16:  --primary: #008060;

Source excerpt (polaris.shopify.com/src/styles/globals.scss, line 16, the
`body { ... }` root custom-property block used for the site's light theme):
    --primary: #008060;

Result: #008060 == #008060 -> EXACT match. Value-preserving.

Capture-method note: the fixture is
`git log -L26,31:polaris.shopify.com/src/components/Button/Button.module.scss
-1 6e958b1c597fcc98dadc2d24d8e7c29a397e20c5` output, not a plain whole-file
`git show <sha> -- <file>`. The real commit (PR #5649, "Use next/link for
components filter") also touches an unrelated hunk earlier in the SAME file
(`display: inline-block;` / `color: inherit;`, a layout/accessibility tweak
with no colour content) plus 6 other files (Button.tsx, ComponentsNav.tsx,
ComponentsNav.module.scss, IconGallery.tsx, Image.tsx, Token.tsx,
utils/various.ts) -- none of which touch this colour pairing. `git log -L`
is a real, first-class git feature (line-history tracking) that produces
byte-real, tool-generated diff output identical in kind to `git show`, just
scoped to a line range instead of a whole file; no text in the hunk below
was hand-typed or edited.

Why -L was needed instead of picking a different, naturally single-hunk
commit: an exhaustive pickaxe scan (single-hex-removed + single-var-added,
per hunk, unbounded context) across 16 non-Lyse OSS design-system repos --
primer/css (exhaustive, all 313 raw `#`-touching scss commits), primer/react,
canvas-kit (already used), twbs/bootstrap (both scss/*.scss and full-repo
*.scss), alphagov/govuk-frontend, salesforce-ux/design-system,
Shopify/polaris (component library AND the polaris.shopify.com subsite),
vmware-clarity/core, adobe/spectrum-css, elastic/eui, thumbtack/thumbprint,
carbon-design-system/carbon-elements, cloudscape-design/components -- found
every OTHER real, single-hunk-for-the-whole-file candidate was a genuine
value MISMATCH, not a pure tokenization:
  - primer/css 6d8e492b: #f3f4f6 removed vs --color-bg-primary: #ffffff
    (@primer/primitives@0.0.0-402d12a) -- different colours.
  - polaris.shopify.com 85a41bed: #fafafa removed vs the effective
    (cascade-final) --surface-subdued: #fbfbfb -- off by one hex digit,
    still a real mismatch.
  - polaris.shopify.com f9a76369: #008362 removed vs --primary: #008060 at
    that commit -- different colours.
  - twbs/bootstrap docs fb212330: #fff removed vs --base02: #c8c8fa (an
    intentional "Fix Chroma line highlight style" recolour, not a pure
    token swap).
Most large DS repos also never hardcode hex directly in component files to
begin with (Bootstrap, EUI, Carbon, Cloudscape, GOV.UK, Thumbprint, SLDS all
came back with zero hex-removed/var-added hunks in their whole scss/css
history) -- they migrate Sass-variable -> CSS-custom-property, never
hex -> CSS-custom-property, so this specific pattern is genuinely much
rarer across the ecosystem than the "abundant" premise assumed. The
6e958b1c/Button.module.scss pairing is the one exact, real,
independently-verified match found; `git log -L` isolates it without
fabricating or editing any diff content.
